{"actor_ref":"claude","class":"draft_history","document_uuid":"01a1a54a-3125-76bd-8a12-3f0211313081","id":"01a1a59e-d1b6-75af-a17c-67027aef0539","idempotency_key":"2b4c4165135618e707c2b1882a94dff735108fcaf132b51762e77f25ace79101","occurred_at":"2026-11-04T08:13:31Z","payload":{"note":"Assistant workspace persistence is complete. Rail: search filters by title and mode, rename writes through /api/ai/sessions/{id}/rename and updates in place, archive removes through the archive route and moves the selection when the open conversation goes away. Restore: the open session is remembered in localStorage (rbc.ai.session) and a reload returns to it; a pinned tab starts on its own conversation ahead of the remembered one. Pin: Workspace gained an optional session_id, activating an assistant workspace opens the Assistant page, and pin_session creates a tab for a conversation or focuses the one it already has, so two sessions can be watched side by side; the tab and its binding persist with the session. Evidence: cargo check --target wasm32-unknown-unknown passes and three browser checks run against the deployed app with a live server -- AI_RAIL_PASS (search 1->0->1, selection survives a page reload), AI_RAIL2_PASS (rename lands in the rail, archive removes exactly one row), AI_PIN_PASS (pin adds one workspace tab and keeps the Assistant view, re-pin focuses it). The checks are committed at apps/rusty-brick-chess/frontend/tests/cdp/ with a README. One note on process: the first AI_RAIL2 run failed on my assertion (expected 0 rows left, there were 2 sessions so 1 was correct) - the code was right and the test was wrong.","target":"t-2883"},"type":"work.note_added","v":2}
